    "fusion_claim_texts": [
      "\n\nBoth checks pass. Done.\n\n## What I did\n\n1. **Loaded the project skill** \u2014 the only skill in `/work/.claude/skills/` is `efficient-coding`; I invoked it via the Skill tool and followed it (small local change \u2192 kept the contract in working memory, single agent, fused edit\u2192validate respecting the dependency).\n2. **Found the bug** in `textkit/slug.py:7-8`. The docstring promises three things \u2014 lowercase, collapse *runs* of non-alphanumerics into a *single* `-`, and strip leading/trailing `-`. The code only did the first, and even the second was wrong: `re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]\", \"-\", text)` replaced **every** non-alphanumeric char (no `+`), and there was no stripping at all.\n3. **Fixed it** to match the docstring:\n   ```python\n   text = re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]+\", \"-\", text)\n   return text.strip(\"-\")\n   ```\n\n## Validation (real exit statuses)\n- **Acceptance check** from your task: `slugify(\"  Hello,   World!  \")` \u2192 `'hello-world'` \u2713\n- **Project test suite**: `pytest tests/test_slug.py` \u2192 `1 passed` \u2713\n\n## Honest close\n- **Changed behavior:** `slugify` now collapses runs of non-alphanumerics to a single dash and strips edge dashes, matching its docstring.\n- **Limitation:** none material. One thing worth noting (not a bug, not changed): the regex is `[a-z0-9]` only, so non-ASCII input like `\"caf\u00e9\"` slugs to `\"caf\"` \u2014 that was pre-existing and outside the requested scope.\n\nCost: no normalized telemetry is available in this environment, so I'm reporting cost as unavailable rather than guessing at savings."
    ]
